Project Background
The current customer authentication experience operates on a legacy platform.
Updating authentication policies or rules on the legacy platform is difficult and often requires coordination across multiple teams.
Project Phoenix will move this capability onto a newer infrastructure where authentication rules and policies can be managed more efficiently.
Phoenix is primarily a capability enabler, not a specific set of new authentication rules.
The ultimate objective is to make the organization more nimble in:
Updating authentication policies
Testing and implementing new rules
Responding to identified customer pain points
Improving authentication outcomes more quickly
Business Case and Funding Need
A business case is needed to support additional funding for Phoenix.
The business case should explain the incremental value Phoenix could enable over the next two to three years.
Potential value areas include:
Reduction in customer complaints
Reduction in customer-service and voice-contact volume
Lower servicing costs
Reduced authentication abandonment
Potential improvements in average handle time
Faster implementation of policy and rule changes
Primary Analysis Requested

The immediate ask is to size the customer complaints associated with authentication and step-up experiences.

The analysis should:

Determine the current annual volume and rate of complaints associated with authentication or step-up experiences.
Establish the overall universe of complaints that could potentially be addressed through future policy improvements enabled by Phoenix.
Categorize the complaints into approximately three major buckets.
Further break the complaints down by the specific authentication experience or failure point, such as:
Authentication failures
Document-verification issues
Other step-up or authentication-related customer experiences
Avoid providing only one aggregate complaint number. The sizing should include a clear category-level breakout.
